 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_CTzdQvyV74XgQhWs",
   "name": "Modulus & Argument",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "894796919"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Modulus & argument"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the modulus of a complex number?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The modulus of a complex number is its **distance **from the origin when plotted on an Argand diagram\n- The modulus of $z$ is written `open vertical bar z close vertical bar`\n- If $z=x+iy$, then we can use **Pythagoras** to show…\n\n  - `open vertical bar z close vertical bar equals square root of x squared plus y squared end root`\n- A modulus is always **positive**\n- the modulus is related to the complex **conjugate** by…\n\n  - `z z to the power of asterisk times equals z to the power of asterisk times z equals open vertical bar z close vertical bar squared`\n  - This is because `z z to the power of asterisk times equals open parentheses x plus straight i y close parentheses open parentheses x minus straight i y close parentheses equals x squared plus y squared`\n- In general, `open vertical bar z subscript 1 plus z subscript 2 close vertical bar not equal to open vertical bar z subscript 1 close vertical bar plus vertical line z subscript 2 vertical line`\n\n  - e.g. both $z_{1}=3+4i$ and $z_{2}=-3+4i$ have a modulus of 5, but $z_{1}+z_{2}$ simplifies to $8i$ which has a modulus of 8"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/06/8-2-3_notes_fig1.png",
   "file": "assets/aa88241b9f44-8-2-3-notes-fig1.png",
   "alt": "8-2-3_notes_fig1",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the argument of a complex number?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The argument of a complex number is the anti-clockwise **angle** that it makes when starting at the positive real axis on an Argand diagram\n- Arguments are measured in **radians**\n\n  - Sometimes these can be given exact in terms of $π$\n- The argument of $z$ is written $\\mathrm{arg}z$\n- Arguments can be calculated using right-angled **trigonometry**\n\n  - This involves using the tan ratio plus a sketch to decide whether it is positive/negative and acute/obtuse\n- Arguments are usually given in the range $-π<\\mathrm{arg}z\\leqπ$\n\n  - Negative arguments are for complex numbers in the third and fourth quadrants\n  - Occasionally you could be asked to give arguments in the range $0\\leq\\mathrm{arg}z<2π$\n- The argument of zero, $\\mathrm{arg}0$ is undefined (no angle can be drawn)"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/06/8-2-3_notes_fig2.png",
   "file": "assets/aeda3338f274-8-2-3-notes-fig2.png",
   "alt": "8-2-3_notes_fig2",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "![8-2-3_example_fig1-part-1](assets/7982bdddd1aa-8-2-3-example-fig1-part-1.png)\n\n![8-2-3_example_fig1-part-2](assets/4f189e455127-8-2-3-example-fig1-part-2.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Give non-exact arguments in radians to 3 significant figures."
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_zp6rDS7x7nWFJG2K",
   "name": "Modulus-Argument (Polar) Form",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "894796865"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Modulus-argument (polar) form"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "The complex number $z=x+iy$ is said to be in Cartesian form. There are, however, other ways to write a complex number, such as in modulus-argument (polar) form."
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I write a complex number in modulus-argument (polar) form?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **Cartesian form **of a complex number, $z=x+iy$, is written in terms of its real part, $x$, and its imaginary part, $y$\n- If we let `r equals vertical line z vertical line` and $θ=\\mathrm{arg}z$, then it is possible to write a complex number in terms of its modulus, $r$, and its argument, $θ$, called the **modulus-argument (polar) form**, given by...\n\n  - `z equals r open parentheses cos space theta plus isin space theta close parentheses`\n- It is usual to give arguments in the range $-π<θ\\leqπ$\n\n  - Negative arguments should be shown clearly, e.g. `z equals 2 open parentheses cos space open parentheses negative pi over 3 close parentheses plus isin space open parentheses negative pi over 3 close parentheses close parentheses`without simplifying `cos invisible function application left parenthesis negative pi over 3 right parenthesis`  to either `cos invisible function application open parentheses pi over 3 close parentheses` or $\\frac{1}{2}$\n  - Occasionally you could be asked to give arguments in the range $0\\leqθ<2π$\n- If a complex number is given in the form `z equals r open parentheses cos space theta minus isin space theta close parentheses`, then it is not currently in modulus-argument (polar) form due to the minus sign, but can be converted as follows…\n\n  - By considering transformations of trigonometric functions, we see that `negative sin invisible function application theta identical to sin invisible function application left parenthesis negative theta right parenthesis` and `cos invisible function application theta identical to cos invisible function application left parenthesis negative theta right parenthesis`\n  - Therefore `z equals r open parentheses cos invisible function application theta minus isin invisible function application theta close parentheses` can be written as `z equals r open parentheses cos invisible function application open parentheses negative theta close parentheses plus isin invisible function application open parentheses negative theta close parentheses close parentheses`, now in the correct form and indicating an argument of $-θ$\n- To convert from modulus-argument (polar) form back to Cartesian form, evaluate the real and imaginary parts\n\n  - E.g. `z equals 2 open parentheses cos invisible function application open parentheses negative pi over 3 close parentheses plus isin invisible function application open parentheses negative pi over 3 close parentheses close parentheses` becomes `z equals 2 open parentheses 1 half plus straight i open parentheses negative fraction numerator square root of 3 over denominator 2 end fraction close parentheses close parentheses equals 1 minus square root of 3 blank straight i`"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/06/8-2-3_notes_fig3.png",
   "file": "assets/bcb7d3943b0c-8-2-3-notes-fig3.png",
   "alt": "8-2-3_notes_fig3",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are the rules for moduli and arguments under multiplication and division?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When two complex numbers, $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$, are **multiplied** to give $z_{1}z_{2}$, their **moduli** are also **multiplied**\n\n  - `open vertical bar z subscript 1 z subscript 2 close vertical bar equals open vertical bar z subscript 1 close vertical bar vertical line z subscript 2 vertical line`\n- When two complex numbers, $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$, are **divided** to give $\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}}$, their **moduli** are also **divided**\n\n  - `open vertical bar z subscript 1 over z subscript 2 close vertical bar equals fraction numerator open vertical bar z subscript 1 close vertical bar over denominator open vertical bar z subscript 2 close vertical bar end fraction`\n- When two complex numbers, $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$, are **multiplied** to give $z_{1}z_{2}$, their **arguments** are **added**\n\n  - `arg space open parentheses z subscript 1 z subscript 2 close parentheses equals arg space z subscript 1 plus arg space z subscript 2`\n- When two complex numbers, $z_{1}$and $z_{2}$, are **divided** to give $\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}}$, their **arguments** are **subtracted**\n\n  - `arg space open parentheses z subscript 1 over z subscript 2 close parentheses equals arg space z subscript 1 minus arg space z subscript 2`"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I multiply complex numbers in modulus-argument (polar) form?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The main benefit of writing complex numbers in modulus-argument (polar) form is that they multiply and divide very easily (often quicker than when in Cartesian form)\n- To **multiply** two complex numbers, $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$, in modulus-argument (polar) form we use the rules from above to **multiply their moduli** and **add their arguments**\n\n  - `open vertical bar z subscript 1 z subscript 2 close vertical bar equals open vertical bar z subscript 1 close vertical bar open vertical bar z subscript 2 close vertical bar`\n  - $\\mathrm{arg}(z_{1}z_{2})=\\mathrm{arg}z_{1}+\\mathrm{arg}z_{2}$\n- So if $z_{1}=r_{1}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{1}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{1})$ and $z_{2}=r_{2}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{2}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{2})$ then the rules above give…\n\n  - `z subscript 1 z subscript 2 equals r subscript 1 r subscript 2 open parentheses cos space open parentheses theta subscript 1 plus theta subscript 2 close parentheses plus isin space open parentheses theta subscript 1 plus theta subscript 2 close parentheses close parentheses blank`\n- Sometimes the new argument, $θ_{1}+θ_{2}$, does not lie in the range $-π<θ\\leqπ$ (or  $0\\leqθ<2π$  if this is being used)\n\n  - An out-of-range argument can be adjusted by either **adding or subtracting **$2π$\n  - E.g. If $θ_{1}=\\frac{2π}{3}$ and $θ_{2}=\\frac{π}{2}$  then  $θ_{1}+θ_{2}=\\frac{7π}{6}$\n  \n    - This is currently not in the range , but by subtracting $2π$ from $\\frac{7π}{6}$ to give $-\\frac{5π}{6}$, a new argument is formed that lies in the correct range and represents the same angle on an Argand diagram\n- The rules of **multiplying the moduli** and **adding the arguments **can also be applied when…\n\n  - …multiplying three complex numbers together, $z_{1}z_{2}z_{3}$, or more\n  - …finding powers of a complex number (e.g. $z^{2}$ can be written as $zz$)\n- Whilst not examinable, the rules for multiplication can be proved algebraically by multiplying $z_{1}=r_{1}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{1}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{1})$ by $z_{2}=r_{2}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{2}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{2})$, expanding the brackets and using compound angle formulae"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I divide complex numbers in modulus-argument (polar) form?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To **divide** two complex numbers, $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$ in modulus-argument (polar) form, we use the rules from above to **divide their moduli** and **subtract their arguments**\n\n  - `open vertical bar z subscript 1 over z subscript 2 close vertical bar blank equals fraction numerator open vertical bar z subscript 1 close vertical bar over denominator vertical line z subscript 2 vertical line end fraction`\n  - `arg space open parentheses z subscript 1 over z subscript 2 close parentheses equals arg space z subscript 1 minus arg space z subscript 2`\n- So if $z_{1}=r_{1}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{1}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{1})$ and $z_{2}=r_{2}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{2}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{2})$ then the rules above give…\n\n  - `z subscript 1 over z subscript 2 equals r subscript 1 over r subscript 2 open parentheses cos space open parentheses theta subscript 1 minus theta subscript 2 close parentheses plus isin space open parentheses theta subscript 1 minus theta subscript 2 close parentheses close parentheses blank`\n- As with multiplication, sometimes the new argument, $θ_{1}-θ_{2}$, can lie out of the range $-π<θ\\leqπ$ (or the range $0\\leqθ<2π$ if this is being used)\n\n  - You can **add or subtract **$2π$ to bring out-of-range arguments back in range\n- Whilst not examinable, the rules for division can be proved algebraically by dividing $z_{1}=r_{1}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{1}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{1})$ by $z_{2}=r_{2}(\\mathrm{cos}θ_{2}+\\mathrm{isin}θ_{2})$, using complex division and compound angle formulae"
